> Usually known as a "Leucrotta."

Although, oddly enough, I've rarely seen it called that. Given that
their origins are in the Middle Ages, one shouldn't be surprised that
they're also called Leucrotas and other variations in spelling.

>  It's a mythical beast of European
> origin with the head of a badger (or lion) and body of a stag.  

Originally supposed to be a cross between a hyena and a lion; they had
no teeth, but a single row of bone instead, and they could mock the
human voice to lure their victims to within striking distance.

> Homunculi are like tiny golems, appearing human apart from their small
> size.

And, indeed, the name means "small human".

> > lich
> 
> Extremely powerful undead.  Resembles a mummy or zombie in that it's
> extremely dessicated and rotten, but moves more gracefully and normally.
> Liches are wizards that have purposefully turned themselves undead to
> attain power and immortality.

Or who have taken possession of another person's dead body to prolong
their own life after their body has succumbed to disease or old age.

>  The concept is likely European in origin, if not in name.

Definitely European, although probably found in other mythologies as
well. The name, however, is just the English version of a common
Germanic word for "body". In Dutch, an ordinary, non-magical body is
called "lijk", and in German, it's called "Leichnam".

> The traditional Lich everyone is familiar with is Gygaxian.  

And Lovecraftian, though I'm not sure he calls it a lich; but it's the
same concept, anyway.

